'''''Hippeastrum puniceum''''' is a [[bulb]]ous [[perennial]] native to tropical regions of [[South America]], although it has become naturalized elsewhere.<ref name=KewWC/> Common names include Barbados lily, Easter lily, cacao lily, cocoa lily and amaryllis lily,{{citation needed|date=October 2011}} although it is neither a lily nor a species of ''[[Amaryllis]]''.<ref name=KewWC/>

==Description==
Plants have 4–6 leaves, each of which is bright green, 30–60&nbsp;cm long by 2.5–3&nbsp;cm wide, strap-shaped (lorate) and tapers at the end to an acute apex. The leaves are not fully developed when the flowers appear (i.e. they are more or less hysteranthous). The flowers are borne in an [[umbel]] on a stem ([[Scape (botany)|scape]]) which is 40–60&nbsp;cm tall. The umbel has lanceolate green bracts at its base. The petals, or more accurately [[tepal]]s, are orange-red with paler bases. The lower two tepals are much narrower than the lateral ones.{{citation needed|date=October 2011}}
